uld have gone  straight through the pagoda that's not a  very good idea but I think in the end  it's actually much more fun to try and  make some custom supports than just  using the in-game ones because you can  kind of make them part of the theme in a  way more interesting way than just these  colored steel beams that you could find  in any Six Flags do so I'll get into  some wooden supporting in a little bit  and talk a bit more about that first I  needed to get a few more walks in here  and there I'm going to do much of the  rockwork kind of spread out over a bunch  of episodes that I'll be having about  this theme just because it's not the  most interesting thing and there's going  to be a lot of rock work and even if  it's interesting for you viewers to  watch it is definitely not interesting  to be doing hours on end so I'm trying  to at least spread that out a little bit  and add as much foliage as I can around  it to to make it look a bit more  interesting and in general if if your  work work will expand in any kind of  particular place it's just like  buildings just add a bit of foliage over  it and it's gonna look good eventually  you just have that corner where the rock  work and it sticks out awkwardly or you  don't really know how to make the shape  look good just put a big Bush in there  and it's fine but yeah finally it's time  for the supports and I wanted to kind of  get some wooden supports in here make it  look like real unique supports instead  of trying to make it look as close to  the in-game supports as I can because  there's no way to actually make it look  like the in-game supports but also this  is a bit more interesting I think since  it has that sort of Mountain Asian well  not all that safe wooden structure to it  which in real life I would probably say  they should be steel supports with a  wooden texture painted on them which is  actually a thing which is really nice  but that's it for this time lapse so I'm  gonna close this video of